      "generated_answer": "We are given that  \n$$\n\\mathbf{a} = \\begin{pmatrix} 1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1 \\end{pmatrix}, \\quad \\mathbf{a} \\cdot \\mathbf{v} = 2, \\quad \\text{and} \\quad \\mathbf{a} \\times \\mathbf{v} = \\begin{pmatrix} 1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1 \\end{pmatrix}.\n$$\n\nLet $\\mathbf{v} = \\begin{pmatrix} x \\\\ y \\\\ z \\end{pmatrix}$. From the dot product:\n$$\n\\mathbf{a} \\cdot \\mathbf{v} = x + y + z = 2.\n$$\n\nFrom the cross product:\n$$\n\\mathbf{a} \\times \\mathbf{v} = \\begin{vmatrix}\n\\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\\n1 & 1 & 1 \\\\\nx & y & z\n\\end{vmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix}\n1\\cdot z - 1\\cdot y \\\\\n1\\cdot x - 1\\cdot z \\\\\n1\\cdot y - 1\\cdot x\n\\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix}\nz - y \\\\\nx - z \\\\\ny - x\n\\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} 1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1 \\end{pmatrix}.\n$$\n\nThis gives the system:\n$$\nz - y = 1, \\quad x - z = -2, \\quad y - x = 1, \\quad x + y + z = 2.\n$$\n\nFrom $z - y = 1$, we get $z = y + 1$.  \nFrom $x - z = -2$, substitute $z$: $x - (y + 1) = -2 \\Rightarrow x = y - 1$.  \nNow substitute into $x + y + z = 2$:\n$$\n(y - 1) + y + (y + 1) = 2 \\Rightarrow 3y = 2 \\Rightarrow y = \\frac{2}{3}.\n$$\n\nThen $x = \\frac{2}{3} - 1 = -\\frac{1}{3}$, $z = \\frac{2}{3} + 1 = \\frac{5}{3}$.\n\nThus,\n$$\n\\mathbf{v} = \\begin{pmatrix} -\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ \\frac{2}{3} \\\\ \\frac{5}{3} \\end{pmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFinal answer:\n\\boxed{\\begin{pmatrix} -\\dfrac{1}{3} \\\\ \\dfrac{2}{3} \\\\ \\dfrac{5}{3} \\end{pmatrix}}",
