    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "To find how many digits repeat in the decimal expansion of $\\frac{31}{11111}$, we note that the length of the repeating part of a fraction $\\frac{a}{b}$ in lowest terms is the smallest $d$ such that $10^d \\equiv 1 \\pmod{b'}$, where $b'$ is the denominator $b$ after removing factors of 2 and 5.\n\nSince 11111 is odd and not divisible by 5, we work directly with 11111. We seek the order of 10 modulo 11111 — the smallest $d$ such that $10^d \\equiv 1 \\pmod{11111}$.\n\nThrough computation or number theory, we find that $11111 = 41 \\times 271$, and the least common multiple of the orders of 10 modulo 41 and modulo 271 is 5, so the repeating decimal has **5** repeating digits.\n\n\\boxed{5}",
