y$, so $z_x = -1, z_y = -1$, so upward normal is $\\langle 1, 1, 1 \\rangle$, yes. So $d\\mathbf{S} = \\langle 1,1,1 \\rangle dx dy$ over the projection.\n\nSo:\n$$\n(\\nabla \\times \\mathbf{F}) \\cdot d\\mathbf{S} = (-y)(1) + (z)(1) + (-x)(1) = -y + z - x = -x - y + (1 - x - y) = 1 - 2x - 2y\n$$\nSame as before.\n\nNow:\n$$\n\\int_0^1 \\int_0^{1-x} (1 - 2x - 2y)\\,dy\\,dx\n$$\nInner:\n$$\n\\int_0^{1-x} (1 - 2x - 2y)\\,dy = \\left[ (1 - 2x)y - y^2 \\right]_0^{1-x} = (1 - 2x)(1 - x) - (1 - x)^2\n$$\nExpand:\n- $(1 - 2x)(1 - x) = 1 - x - 2x + 2x^2 = 1 - 3x + 2x^2$\n- $(1 - x)^2 = 1 - 2x + x^2$\n- Difference: $ (1 - 3x + 2x^2) - (1 - 2x + x^2) = -x + x^2 $\n\nIntegrate:\n$$\n\\int_0^1 (-x + x^2)\\,dx = \\left[ -\\frac{1}{2}x^2 + \\frac{1}{3}x^3 \\right]_0^1 = -\\frac{1}{2} + \\frac{1}{3} = -\\frac{1}{6}\n$$\nBut $-\\frac{1}{6}$ is not among the options.\n\nWait — this suggests an error. Let’s recheck the cross product.\n\n$$\n\\nabla \\times \\mathbf{F} = \\begin{vmatrix}\n\\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\\n\\partial_x & \\partial_y & \\partial_z \\\\\nxy & yz & zx\n\\end{vmatrix}\n= \\mathbf{i} \\left( \\frac{\\partial (zx)}{\\partial y} - \\frac{\\partial (yz)}{\\partial z} \\right) - \\mathbf{j} \\left( \\frac{\\partial (zx)}{\\partial x} - \\frac{\\partial (xy)}{\\partial z} \\right) + \\mathbf{k} \\left( \\frac{\\partial (yz)}{\\partial x} - \\frac{\\partial (xy)}{\\partial y} \\right)\n$$\n\nCompute:\n- $\\frac{\\partial (zx)}{\\partial y} = 0$, $\\frac{\\partial (yz)}{\\partial z} = y$ → $0 - y = -y$ → $\\mathbf{i}(-y)$\n- $\\frac{\\partial (zx)}{\\partial x} = z$, $\\frac{\\partial (xy)}{\\partial z} = 0$ → so $- \\mathbf{j}(z - 0) = -z \\mathbf{j}$\n- $\\frac{\\partial (yz)}{\\partial x} = 0$, $\\frac{\\partial (xy)}{\\partial y} = x$ → $0 - x = -x$ → $-x \\mathbf{k}$\n\nSo $\\nabla \\times \\mathbf{F} = \\langle -y, -z, -x \\rangle$? Wait — sign on $\\mathbf{j}$: it's **minus** the determinant, so:\n$$\n\\nabla \\times \\mathbf{F} = \\mathbf{i}(-y) - \\mathbf{j}(z - 0) + \\mathbf{k}(-x) = \\langle -y, -z, -x \\rangle\n$$\nWait no:\n\nStandard determinant:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{i} \\left( \\partial_y(zx) - \\partial_z(yz) \\right) = \\mathbf{i}(0 - y) = -y \\mathbf{i}\n$$\n$$\n\\mathbf{j} \\left( \\partial_z(xy) - \\partial_x(zx) \\right) = \\mathbf{j}(0 - z) = -z \\mathbf{j} \\quad \\text{but with negative sign in determinant → becomes } -(-z)\\mathbf{j}?  \n$$\nNo — the formula is:\n$$\n(\\partial_y F_z - \\partial_z F_y)\\mathbf{i} - (\\partial_x F_z - \\partial_z F_x)\\mathbf{j} + (\\partial_x F_y - \\partial_y F_x)\\mathbf{k}\n$$\nSo:\n- $F_x = xy$, $F_y = yz$, $F_z = zx$\n\nSo:\n- $ \\partial_y F_z = \\partial_y(zx) = 0 $, $ \\partial_z F_y = \\partial_z(yz) = y $ → $0 - y = -y $\n- $ \\partial_x F_z = \\partial_x(zx) = z $, $ \\partial_z F_x = \\partial_z(xy) = 0 $ → $ z - 0 = z $ → minus this: $ -z $\n- $ \\partial_x F_y = \\partial_x(yz) = 0 $, $ \\partial_y F_x = \\partial_y(xy) = x $ → $0 - x = -x$\n\nSo:\n$$\n\\nabla \\times \\mathbf{F} = (-y)\\mathbf{i} - (z)\\mathbf{j} + (-x)\\mathbf{k} = \\langle -y, -z, -x \\rangle\n$$\nEarlier I had $\\langle -y, z, -x \\rangle$, which was wrong. Correct is $\\langle -y, -z, -x \\rangle$\n\nNow dot with $d\\mathbf{S} = \\langle 1,1,1 \\rangle dx dy$, so:\n$$\n(\\nabla \\times \\mathbf{F}) \\cdot d\\mathbf{S} = (-y)(1) + (-z)(1) + (-x)(1) = -x - y - z\n$$\nBut on the plane $x + y + z = 1$, so $-x - y - z = -1$\n\nSo integrand is $-1$ over the triangle.\n\nNow area of projection: $x \\ge 0, y \\ge 0, x+y \\le 1$, area is $\\frac{1}{2}$\n\nSo:\n$$\n\\iint_S -1\\,dA = -1 \\times \\text{Area} = -1 \\times \\frac{1}{2} = -\\frac{1}{2}\n$$\nThus, the value is $-\\frac{1}{2}$\n\nCorrect answer is A.\n\n\\boxed{A}",
