      "generated_answer": "Stoke's Theorem states that $\\int_C \\vec{F} \\cdot d\\vec{r} = \\iint_S (\\nabla \\times \\vec{F}) \\cdot d\\vec{S}$, where $S$ is a surface bounded by $C$. First, compute $\\nabla \\times \\vec{F}$:  \n$$\n\\nabla \\times \\vec{F} = \\begin{vmatrix}\n\\vec{i} & \\vec{j} & \\vec{k} \\\\\n\\partial/\\partial x & \\partial/\\partial y & \\partial/\\partial z \\\\\nz^2 & y^2 & x\n\\end{vmatrix} = (0 - 0)\\vec{i} - (1 - 2z)\\vec{j} + (0 - 0)\\vec{k} = (2z - 1)\\vec{j}.\n$$  \nThe surface $S$ is the plane $x + y + z = 1$ in the first octant; a normal vector is $\\vec{n} = \\langle 1,1,1 \\rangle$, and $d\\vec{S} = \\vec{n}\\,dA = \\langle 1,1,1 \\rangle\\,dx\\,dy$. Projecting onto the $xy$-plane, $z = 1 - x - y$, so $(\\nabla \\times \\vec{F}) = (2(1 - x - y) - 1)\\vec{j} = (1 - 2x - 2y)\\vec{j}$. Then:\n$$\n(\\nabla \\times \\vec{F}) \\cdot d\\vec{S} = (1 - 2x - 2y)(1)\\,dx\\,dy.\n$$\nIntegrate over the triangle $x \\ge 0, y \\ge 0, x + y \\le 1$:\n$$\n\\int_0^1 \\int_0^{1-x} (1 - 2x - 2y)\\,dy\\,dx = \\int_0^1 \\left[(1 - 2x)y - y^2\\right]_0^{1-x} dx = \\int_0^1 (1 - 2x)(1 - x) - (1 - x)^2 dx.\n$$\nSimplify:\n$$\n= \\int_0^1 (1 - x - 2x + 2x^2) - (1 - 2x + x^2) dx = \\int_0^1 (1 - 3x + 2x^2 - 1 + 2x - x^2) dx = \\int_0^1 (-x + x^2) dx = \\left[-\\frac{1}{2}x^2 + \\frac{1}{3}x^3\\right]_0^1 = -\\frac{1}{2} + \\frac{1}{3} = -\\frac{1}{6} \\approx -0.166.\n$$\n\nThus, the correct answer is $\\boxed{C}$.",
