    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Find the sum of $\\sum_{{n=1}}^{{\\infty}} \\frac{{2}}{{n^2 + 4n + 3}}$\n\nOptions: \nA. 1.6333\nB. 0.9333\nC. 2.0333\nD. 0.7333\nE. 0.8333\nF. 1.4333\nG. 2.1433\nH. 0.4333\nI. 0.5633\nJ. 1.2333\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "We start by factoring the denominator:  \n$n^2 + 4n + 3 = (n+1)(n+3)$.  \n\nSo the general term becomes:  \n$\\frac{2}{(n+1)(n+3)}$.  \n\nUsing partial fractions:  \n$\\frac{2}{(n+1)(n+3)} = \\frac{A}{n+1} + \\frac{B}{n+3}$.  \nSolving: $2 = A(n+3) + B(n+1)$.  \nSet $n = -1$: $2 = A(2) \\Rightarrow A = 1$.  \nSet $n = -3$: $2 = B(-2) \\Rightarrow B = -1$.  \n\nThus:  \n$\\frac{2}{(n+1)(n+3)} = \\frac{1}{n+1} - \\frac{1}{n+3}$.  \n\nNow sum from $n=1$ to $\\infty$:  \n$\\sum_{n=1}^{\\infty} \\left( \\frac{1}{n+1} - \\frac{1}{n+3} \\right)$.  \n\nThis is a telescoping series. Writing out terms:  \n$\\left( \\frac{1}{2} - \\frac{1}{4} \\right) + \\left( \\frac{1}{3} - \\frac{1}{5} \\right) + \\left( \\frac{1}{4} - \\frac{1}{6} \\right) + \\left( \\frac{1}{5} - \\frac{1}{7} \\right) + \\cdots$  \n\nMost terms cancel. The surviving terms are:  \n$\\frac{1}{2} + \\frac{1}{3} = \\frac{5}{6} \\approx 0.8333$.  \n\nThus, the sum is $ \\frac{5}{6} = 0.8333 $.  \n\n\\boxed{E}",
