    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Find $\\gamma$ so that the solution of the initial value problem $x^2 y^{{\\prime \\prime}}-2 y=0, y(1)=1, y^{{\\prime}}(1)=\\gamma$ is bounded as $x \\rightarrow 0$.\n\nOptions: \nA. 6\nB. 5\nC. 1/2\nD. 1\nE. 0\nF. 3\nG.  2\nH. -2\nI. -1\nJ. 4\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "The differential equation $ x^2 y'' - 2y = 0 $ is a Cauchy-Euler equation. We assume a solution of the form $ y = x^r $, leading to the characteristic equation $ r(r-1) - 2 = 0 $, or $ r^2 - r - 2 = 0 $, which factors as $ (r - 2)(r + 1) = 0 $. Thus, $ r = 2 $ or $ r = -1 $, giving general solution $ y(x) = C_1 x^2 + C_2 x^{-1} $.\n\nAs $ x \\to 0 $, the term $ x^{-1} $ blows up unless $ C_2 = 0 $. To have a bounded solution, we must eliminate the singular term, so $ C_2 = 0 $, leaving $ y(x) = C_1 x^2 $. Using the initial condition $ y(1) = 1 $, we get $ C_1 = 1 $, so $ y(x) = x^2 $. Then $ y'(x) = 2x $, and $ y'(1) = 2 $. Thus, $ \\gamma = 2 $.\n\n\\boxed{G}",
