    "global index": "MMLUPro_math_7254",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Let I=[0,1]\\times[0,1]. Suppose $E={{(x, y) \\in I: sin(x)<\\frac{{1}}{{2}}, cos(x+y) is irrational}}$, what is the Lebesgue measure of E?\n\nOptions: \nA. 0.7853981634\nB. 1.0471975512\nC. 1.8849555922\nD. 0.6283185307\nE. 0.2617993833\nF. 2.0943951024\nG. 1.5707963268\nH. 0.5235987667\nI. 0.3926990817\nJ. 1.3089969390\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "The set $ E = \\{(x, y) \\in [0,1] \\times [0,1] : \\sin(x) < \\frac{1}{2} \\text{ and } \\cos(x+y) \\text{ is irrational}\\} $ involves two conditions. The set where $ \\sin(x) < \\frac{1}{2} $ corresponds to $ x < \\frac{\\pi}{6} $ or $ x > \\frac{5\\pi}{6} $, but within $[0,1]$, since $ \\frac{\\pi}{6} \\approx 0.5236 $ and $ 1 < \\frac{5\\pi}{6} $, only $ x \\in [0, \\frac{\\pi}{6}) $ applies approximately. However, $ \\cos(x+y) $ is irrational almost everywhere (since rationals are countable), so this condition contributes full measure where defined. The dominant constraint is $ \\sin(x) < \\frac{1}{2} $, which on $[0,1]$ holds for $ x \\in [0, \\arcsin(1/2)] = [0, \\pi/6] \\approx 0.5236 $. So the measure is approximately $ (\\pi/6) \\times 1 = \\pi/6 \\approx 0.5236 $.\n\nThus, the answer is:\n\n\\boxed{H}",
