      "generated_answer": "The expansion of $(m+n)^6$ has 7 terms (from $\\binom{6}{0}$ to $\\binom{6}{6}$). Since $m$ and $n$ are odd, their sum $m+n$ is even, but we are expanding powers, and each term is of the form $\\binom{6}{k} m^{6-k} n^k$. Because $m$ and $n$ are odd, $m^{6-k}n^k$ is odd, so the parity of each term depends only on $\\binom{6}{k}$: the term is odd if and only if $\\binom{6}{k}$ is odd.\n\nUsing Lucas’ theorem or direct computation, $\\binom{6}{k}$ is odd for $k = 0, 2, 4, 6$, so 4 of the 7 terms are odd.\n\n$$\\boxed{D}$$",
