      "generated_answer": "The problem asks whether we can construct a cube with volume $X$ times a given cube using compass and straightedge. This requires constructing a length equal to $\\sqrt[3]{X}$, which is possible only if $\\sqrt[3]{X}$ is a constructible number. A number is constructible only if it can be expressed using square roots, meaning $\\sqrt[3]{X}$ must lie in a field extension of degree a power of 2; thus, $X$ must not require cube roots that aren’t reducible to square roots.\n\nPerfect cubes like 8 ($2^3$), 27 ($3^3$), 343 ($7^3$), and 1331 ($11^3$) have cube roots 2, 3, 7, and 11, which are rational and hence constructible. However, $\\sqrt[3]{3}$ and $\\sqrt[3]{17}$ are not constructible because they involve cube roots of non-perfect cubes and cannot be simplified to square roots. So for $X = 3, 17$: 0; for $X = 8, 27, 343, 1331$: 1.\n\nThe correct answer is [0, 0, 1, 1, 1, 1], which corresponds to option C.\n\n\\boxed{C}",
