    "prediction": "qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "We are given that $ f $ is analytic on the closed unit disk, $ f(0) = 0 $, and $ |\\operatorname{Re} f(z)| \\leq |e^z| $ for $ |z| < 1 $. Note that $ |e^z| = e^{\\operatorname{Re}(z)} $, and for $ |z| < 1 $, this is bounded by $ e^{|z|} < e $. However, the key idea is to use bounds on analytic functions with controlled real parts. By the Borel-Carathéodory inequality, if $ \\operatorname{Re} f(z) $ is bounded by $ M $, then $ |f(z)| $ can be bounded in terms of $ M $ and $ |z| $. Given $ |\\operatorname{Re} f(z)| \\leq e^{\\operatorname{Re}(z)} \\leq e^1 = e $ on $ |z| < 1 $, Borel-Carathéodory gives $ |f(z)| \\leq \\frac{2r}{1 - r} e $ for $ |z| = r $. At $ z = \\frac{1+i}{2} $, we have $ |z| = \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\approx 0.707 $. Plugging in $ r = \\sqrt{2}/2 $, we estimate $ |f(z)| \\leq \\frac{2 \\cdot 0.707}{1 - 0.707} \\cdot e \\approx \\frac{1.414}{0.293} \\cdot 2.718 \\approx 4.826 \\cdot 2.718 \\approx 13.12 $, which suggests the maximum value is less than 14.\n\nAmong the options, the only one below 14 is **F. 13.45**. Although our bound is rough, it supports this choice as the most reasonable.\n\n\\boxed{F}",
