      "generated_answer": "We analyze the degrees in the expression $ f(g(x)) + g(h(x)) + h(f(x)) $. Since $ f $ has degree 6 and $ g $ has degree 3, $ f(g(x)) $ has degree $ 6 \\times 3 = 18 $. Next, $ g(h(x)) $ has degree $ 3 \\times \\deg(h) $, and $ h(f(x)) $ has degree $ \\deg(h) \\times 6 $. To get a total degree of 36, the highest degree term must come from either $ g(h(x)) $ or $ h(f(x)) $. The maximum of $ 3\\deg(h) $ and $ 6\\deg(h) $ must be 36, so $ 6\\deg(h) = 36 \\Rightarrow \\deg(h) = 6 $, but then $ f(g(x)) $ only contributes degree 18, and $ h(f(x)) $ would be $ 6 \\times 6 = 36 $, which works. However, if $ \\deg(h) = 9 $, then $ g(h(x)) = 3 \\times 9 = 27 $, $ h(f(x)) = 9 \\times 6 = 54 $, too big. Only $ \\deg(h) = 6 $ gives one term at degree 36. Wait — but $ h(f(x)) = \\deg(h) \\times 6 $. To reach degree 36, $ \\deg(h) \\times 6 = 36 \\Rightarrow \\deg(h) = 6 $. But then $ f(g(x)) $ is degree 18, $ h(f(x)) $ is degree 36, $ g(h(x)) = 3 \\times 6 = 18 $. So maximum is 36, good. But is there a higher possibility? Suppose $ \\deg(h) = 12 $, then $ h(f(x)) = 12 \\times 6 = 72 $, too big. But the resulting polynomial is only degree 36, so the highest degree term must be exactly 36. So $ h(f(x)) $ must be degree $ \\deg(h) \\times 6 \\leq 36 $, so $ \\deg(h) \\leq 6 $. But also, some term must reach 36. So $ h(f(x)) $ must be degree 36, so $ \\deg(h) = 6 $. But wait — unless $ g(h(x)) $ is degree 36: $ 3 \\times \\deg(h) = 36 \\Rightarrow \\deg(h) = 12 $. Then $ h(f(x)) = 12 \\times 6 = 72 $, which would make the whole expression degree 72, not 36. So to avoid exceeding 36, the dominant term must be exactly 36, and no term can exceed it. So if $ \\deg(h) = 6 $, $ h(f(x)) = 36 $, others are less: okay. But if $ \\deg(h) = 6 $, then $ g(h(x)) = 3 \\times 6 = 18 $, fine. So only $ h(f(x)) $ gives degree 36. That works. But check: if $ \\deg(h) = 9 $, $ h(f(x)) = 54 > 36 $, too big. So only possibility is $ \\deg(h) = 6 $. But wait — what if the degree comes from $ g(h(x)) $? Then $ 3 \\deg(h) = 36 \\Rightarrow \\deg(h) = 12 $. Then $ h(f(x)) = 12 \\times 6 = 72 $, which dominates, so total degree would be 72, not 36. So that can't happen. Thus, the only way to have degree 36 is if $ h(f(x)) $ contributes it: $ \\deg(h) \\times 6 = 36 \\Rightarrow \\deg(h) = 6 $, and other terms must not exceed 36. $ f(g(x)) = 6 \\times 3 = 18 $, $ g(h(x)) = 3 \\times 6 = 18 $, both less. So maximum is 36. So $ \\deg(h) = 6 $.\n\n\\boxed{C}",
