      "generated_answer": "We are asked to count the number of distinct ways to color the faces of a cube using three colors, where two colorings are considered the same if one can be rotated to look like the other. This is a classic application of **Burnside's Lemma**, which counts the number of distinct colorings under group actions (here, the rotation group of the cube).\n\nApplying Burnside’s Lemma and averaging the number of colorings fixed by each of the 24 rotational symmetries of the cube, the correct number of distinct colorings with 3 colors is **57**.\n\n\\boxed{E}",
