    "global index": "MMLUPro_math_6816",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: V is a vector space over the real field R. It is known that the vector group u_1, u_2, u_3 in V are linearly independent. Finding the rank of vector group ${{u_1-\\lambda u_2, u_2-\\lambda u_3, u_3-\\lambda u_1}}$ for $\\lambda=\\sqrt{{5}}$ and $\\lambda=1$ separately. Return the answer as a list.\n\nOptions: \nA. [1, 2]\nB. [2, 1]\nC. [3, 2]\nD. [1, 1]\nE. [2, 3]\nF. [3, 1]\nG. [3, 3]\nH. [1, 3]\nI. [2, 2]\nJ. [0, 2]\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "We are given that $ u_1, u_2, u_3 $ are linearly independent vectors in a vector space $ V $ over $ \\mathbb{R} $, and we are to find the rank of the vector group:\n$$\n\\{u_1 - \\lambda u_2, u_2 - \\lambda u_3, u_3 - \\lambda u_1\\}\n$$\nfor $ \\lambda = \\sqrt{5} $ and $ \\lambda = 1 $, respectively.\n\nLet us define the vectors:\n$$\nv_1 = u_1 - \\lambda u_2, \\quad v_2 = u_2 - \\lambda u_3, \\quad v_3 = u_3 - \\lambda u_1.\n$$\nWe want to determine whether $ v_1, v_2, v_3 $$\n are linearly independent. Consider a linear combination:\n$$\na v_1 + b v_2 + c v_3 = 0.\n$$\nSubstitute:\n$$\na(u_1 - \\lambda u_2) + b(u_2 - \\lambda u_3) + c(u_3 - \\lambda u_1) = 0.\n$$\nGrouping terms:\n$$\na u_1 - a\\lambda u_2 + b u_2 - b\\lambda u_3 + c u_3 - c\\lambda u_1 = (a - c\\lambda)u_1 + (-a\\lambda + b)u_2 + (-b\\lambda + c)u_3 = 0.\n$$\nSince $ u_1, u_2, u_3 $ are linearly independent, coefficients must be zero:\n$$\n\\begin{cases}\na - c\\lambda = 0 \\\\\n-\\lambda a + b = 0 \\\\\n-\\lambda b + c = 0\n\\end{cases}\n\\Rightarrow\n\\begin{cases}\na = \\lambda c \\\\\nb = \\lambda a = \\lambda^2 c \\\\\nc = \\lambda b = \\lambda^3 c\n\\end{cases}\n$$\nFrom last equation: $ c = \\lambda^3 c \\Rightarrow c(1 - \\lambda^3) = 0 $.\n\n- If $ \\lambda^3 \\ne 1 $, then $ c = 0 $, so $ a = 0, b = 0 $: only trivial solution → vectors are linearly independent → rank 3.\n- If $ \\lambda^3 = 1 $, then nontrivial solutions possible → rank < 3.\n\nNow evaluate for given values:\n\n1. $ \\lambda = \\sqrt{5} $: $ \\lambda^3 = 5\\sqrt{5} \\ne 1 $, so only trivial solution → rank = 3.\n2. $ \\lambda = 1 $: $ \\lambda^3 = 1 $, so $ c(1 - 1) = 0 $ → $ c $ arbitrary.\n\nThen from $ a = \\lambda c = c $, $ b = \\lambda^2 c = c $, and $ c = c $. So nontrivial solution exists (e.g., $ a = b = c = 1 $).\n\nCheck: $ v_1 + v_2 + v_3 = (u_1 - u_2) + (u_2 - u_3) + (u_3 - u_1) = 0 $. So they are linearly dependent.\n\nBut are any two independent?\n\nCheck $ v_1 = u_1 - u_2 $, $ v_2 = u_2 - u_3 $. Suppose $ a v_1 + b v_2 = 0 $:\n$$\na(u_1 - u_2) + b(u_2 - u_3) = a u_1 + (-a + b)u_2 - b u_3 = 0.\n$$\nThen $ a = 0 $, $ -a + b = 0 $, $ -b = 0 $ → $ a = b = 0 $. So $ v_1, v_2 $ are independent.\n\nThus for $ \\lambda = 1 $, the rank is 2.\n\nSo the ranks are: $ [3, 2] $ for $ \\lambda = \\sqrt{5}, 1 $.\n\nAnswer: **C. [3, 2]**\n\n\\boxed{C}",
