  {
    "global index": "MMLUPro_physics_7868",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Find the value of the integral $\\int_S(\\nabla \\times \\mathbf{{A}}) \\cdot d \\mathbf{{a}}$ if the vector $\\mathbf{{A}}=y \\mathbf{{i}}+z \\mathbf{{j}}+x \\mathbf{{k}}$ and $S$ is the surface defined by the paraboloid $z=1-x^2-y^2$, where $z \\geq 0$.\n\nOptions: \nA. $2\\pi$\nB. $\\pi$\nC. $\\frac{{\\pi}}{{2}}$\nD. $4\\pi$\nE. $-\\frac{{\\pi}}{{2}}$\nF. $-2\\pi$\nG. $3\\pi$\nH. $0$\nI. $-\\pi$\nJ. $-3\\pi$\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "We apply Stokes’ theorem:  \n$$\n\\int_S (\\nabla \\times \\mathbf{A}) \\cdot d\\mathbf{a} = \\oint_{\\partial S} \\mathbf{A} \\cdot d\\mathbf{r}\n$$\nThe boundary $\\partial S$ is the circle $x^2 + y^2 = 1$ in the $z = 0$ plane. With $\\mathbf{A} = y\\mathbf{i} + z\\mathbf{j} + x\\mathbf{k}$, on $z = 0$, we have $\\mathbf{A} = y\\mathbf{i} + x\\mathbf{k}$. Parametrize the boundary as $\\mathbf{r}(t) = \\cos t\\,\\mathbf{i} + \\sin t\\,\\mathbf{j}$, $0 \\leq t \\leq 2\\pi$, so $d\\mathbf{r} = (-\\sin t\\,\\mathbf{i} + \\cos t\\,\\mathbf{j})dt$. Then $\\mathbf{A} \\cdot d\\mathbf{r} = (\\sin t)(-\\sin t)\\,dt + 0 = -\\sin^2 t\\,dt$.  \n$$\n\\oint_{\\partial S} \\mathbf{A} \\cdot d\\mathbf{r} = \\int_0^{2\\pi} -\\sin^2 t\\,dt = -\\int_0^{2\\pi} \\frac{1 - \\cos 2t}{2} dt = -\\frac{1}{2}(2\\pi) = -\\pi\n$$\n\nThus, the value of the integral is $-\\pi$.\n\n\\boxed{I}",
      "success": true,
      "token_usage": {
        "input_tokens": 243,
        "output_tokens": 376,
        "total_tokens": 619
      },
      "provider": "openrouter",
