en, etc. So sum = odd + even = odd. $ n $ is odd. Now $ \\mod 4 $: 1! =1, 2! =2, 3! =6≡2, 4! =24≡0, 5! and above are divisible by 4! =24, so divisible by 4 → ≡0 mod 4. So total sum $ \\mod 4 $: 1+2+2+0+0+... = 5 ≡ 1 \\mod 4. So $ n \\equiv 1 \\pmod{4} $. So $ 3^1 = 3 \\pmod{10} $. So last digit is 3.\n\nBut let me check actual small: if n=3, 3^3=27→7. But here n is large, ends in 3, exponent ≡1 mod 4 → 3^1=3, 3^5=243→3, yes.\n\nSo answer should be 3.\n\nBut why do I recall that such sums usually end with 3 and power ends with 3?\n\nWait — but let me recalculate the sum mod 10:\n\n1! = 1  \n2! = 2  \n3! = 6  \n4! = 24 → 4  \n5! = 120 → 0  \n6! = 720 → 0  \n7! = 5040 → 0  \n8! = 40320 → 0  \n9! = 362880 → 0  \n10! = 3628800 → 0\n\nSum of last digits: 1+2+6+4+0+0+0+0+0+0 = 13 → last digit 3. Correct.\n\nSo n ends in 3, and exponent n ≡1 mod 4, so n^n ends in 3^1 = 3.\n\nBut 3^3 = 27 ends in 7, but only if exponent is 3, not if exponent is 1 mod 4.\n\nBut here exponent ≡1 mod 4, so for base ending in 3, the cycle is:  \nexponent 1: 3  \n2: 9  \n3: 7  \n4: 1  \n5: 3  \nSo yes, if exponent ≡1 mod 4, last digit is 3.\n\nSince n ≡1 mod 4, so last digit of 3^n is 3.\n\nSo answer is 3.\n\nBut let me check the total sum modulo 4 again.\n\nMod 4:  \n1! = 1  \n2! = 2  \n3! = 6 ≡ 2 mod 4  \n4! = 24 ≡ 0  \n5! = 120 ≡ 0  \nAnd all higher ≡0 mod 4\n\nSum: 1+2+2+0+0+0... = 5 ≡ 1 mod 4. Yes.\n\nSo exponent n ≡1 mod 4.\n\nSo $ n^n \\mod 10 = 3^1 = 3 \\mod 10 $. Last digit is 3.\n\nTherefore, the answer is A. 3.\n\n\\boxed{A}",
