    "global index": "MathQA_1650",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: ram and shyam start a two - length swimming race at the same moment but from opposite ends of the pool . they swim in lanes at uniform speeds , but hardy is faster than andy . they 1 st pass at a point 18.5 m from the deep end and having completed one length each 1 is allowed to rest on the edge for exactly 45 sec . after setting off on the return length , the swimmers pass for the 2 nd time just 10.5 m from the shallow end . how long is the pool ?\n\nOptions: \nA. 65\nB. 60\nC. 55\nD. 50\nE. 45\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "Let the length of the pool be $ L $ meters. When Ram and Shyam first meet, they are 18.5 m from the deep end, so the sum of the distances they've swum is $ L $. Since they started from opposite ends, and one is faster than the other, the ratio of their speeds can be determined from this. On the second meeting, after each has rested 45 seconds (which affects neither speed nor ratio), they meet again 10.5 m from the shallow end, meaning together they have swum $ 3L $ in total by then. Using relative distances: $ 18.5 + 10.5 = L $ implies $ L = 50 $, but more careful analysis of ratios gives $ L = 55 $ as the consistent solution.\n\nSolving via relative speed ratios: first meeting at 18.5 from deep end, second at 10.5 from shallow end → $ L = 18.5 + (L - 10.5) \\times \\frac{v_1}{v_2} $, and symmetry leads to $ L = 55 $.\n\n\\boxed{C}",
      "success": true,
      "token_usage": {
        "input_tokens": 212,
        "output_tokens": 253,
