    "global index": "MathQA_1395",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: if c and t are positive integers , ct + c + t can not be\n\nOptions: \nA. 5\nB. 6\nC. 7\nD. 8\nE. 9\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "We analyze the expression $ ct + c + t $. Adding 1 to it gives $ ct + c + t + 1 = (c+1)(t+1) $, which must be an integer greater than 1 since $ c, t $ are positive integers. So $ ct + c + t = (c+1)(t+1) - 1 $. The values $ (c+1)(t+1) $ are composite or at least 4 (since $ c+1 \\geq 2, t+1 \\geq 2 $), so $ ct + c + t $ must be one less than a composite number. Checking values:  \n- 5 + 1 = 6 = 2×3 → possible  \n- 6 + 1 = 7 → prime → not possible as (c+1)(t+1) cannot be prime  \nSo 6 cannot be expressed in the form.\n\n\\boxed{B}",
