      "generated_answer": "We are given the joint density $ f(x, y) = x + y $ for $ 0 < x < 1 $, $ 0 < y < 1 $. To find the conditional expectation $ E[Y \\mid X = x] $, we first compute the marginal $ f_X(x) = \\int_0^1 (x + y) \\, dy = x + \\frac{1}{2} $. The conditional density is $ f_{Y|X}(y|x) = \\frac{x + y}{x + \\frac{1}{2}} $, and then $ E[Y \\mid X = x] = \\int_0^1 y \\cdot \\frac{x + y}{x + \\frac{1}{2}} \\, dy $. Computing this gives $ \\frac{3x + 2}{3(2x + 1)} $, which matches option G.\n\n\\boxed{G}",
