s 1/5.125e-6 = 195.121 kHz.\n\n195.121 / 8.8 ≈ 22.17, not 4.\n\nWait, perhaps \" four times the Nyquist rate\" is misinterpreted.\n\nNyquist rate = 2* f_max = 8.8 kHz.\n\nBut sampled at four times = 35.2 kHz.\n\nBut maybe they mean the sampling interval is T, and they want T.\n\nBut no match.\n\nPerhaps the range is 0.4 to 4.4 kHz, so bandwidth B = 4 kHz, and for sampling, if they use baseband treatment, Nyquist rate is 2*4.4 = 8.8 kHz.\n\nBut maybe the signal is complex sampled or something.\n\nPerhaps \" four times the Nyquist rate\" means the sampling rate is 4 * f_max = 4*4.4 = 17.6 kHz, then T = 56.8 µs — not in options.\n\nOr 4 * (f_U - f_L) = 4*4 = 16 kHz, T = 62.5 µs.\n\nNo.\n\nLet's look at the options and see which one has N=8 and reasonable P_e.\n\nN=8 bits for 256 levels, so N=8.\n\nSo options: B, F, J.\n\nFor (c) P_e for 256 orthogonal signals.\n\nFor M=256 orthogonal signals, in AWGN, with coherent detection, the symbol error probability is:\n\nP_e = 1 - \\int_{-\\infty}^{\\infty} [1 - Q(x)]^{ \\sqrt{2E_s/N_0} } \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2\\pi}} e^{- (x - \\sqrt{2E_s/N_0})^2 /2 } dx — complicated.\n\nA tight bound is P_e < (M-1) e^{- E_s / (4N_0)} / 2\n\nOr approximately, for large E_s/N_0, P_e ≈ (2^{k} - 1) / 2 * Q( sqrt(0.5 * 2^{k} E_b / N_0) ) but it's messy.\n\nStandard result: for M-ary orthogonal signaling, the probability of symbol error is approximately\n\nP_e ≈ 2^{k-1} Q( sqrt( k E_b / N_0 ) ) for large k, but not accurate.\n\nAnother way: the energy per bit E_b = E_s / k, k = log2(M) = 8.\n\nE_s / N_0 = (s_i * T) / η = (s_i / η) * T = 10^6 * T\n\nSo E_s / N_0 = 10^6 * T\n\nThen E_b / N_0 = (E_s / N_0) / 8 = 10^6 * T / 8\n\nFor orthogonal signaling, the error probability is approximately:\n\nP_e ≈ Q( sqrt( (2^k - 1) E_b / (2 N_0) ) ) for k=1,2, but for large M, it's different.\n\nFor large M, the capacity-achieving signaling is orthogonal, but error probability is:\n\nA better approximation: P_e = (M-1) / 2 * erfc( sqrt( E_s / (4 N_0) ) ) for non-coherent, but for coherent, it's:\n\nFor coherent detection of orthogonal signals, the probability of error is:\n\nP_e = 1 - \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{\\pi^M}} \\int \\ etc. \n\nBut a common approximation is that P_e is small when  E_s / N_0 >> log M.\n\nBut let's try to match the numbers.\n\nSuppose P_e = 10^{-5} in option B.\n\nWhat T is needed?\n\nFrom E_s / N_0 = 10^6 * T\n\nFor M=256, to have P_e = 10^{-5}, what E_s / N_0 is needed?\n\nFrom tables or approximations, for 16-ary orthogonal, at P_e=10^{-5}, E_s/N_0 is about 7 dB = 5.\n\nFor 256-ary, it's higher.\n\nIn fact, for M-ary orthogonal, P_e decreases as M increases for fixed E_s/N_0, because more immunity.\n\nFor example, for M=2 (orthogonal), P_e = Q( sqrt(2E_s/N_0) ) since orthogonal BFSK.\n\nFor M=2, two orthogonal signals, P_e = Q( sqrt(2E_s/N_0) ) for coherent detection? No, for non-coherent, P_e = Q( sqrt(E_s/N_0) ).\n\nFor coherent detection of orthogonal signals, the decision is based on which correlator has higher output.\n\nFor two signals, if s1 and s2 are orthogonal, then P_e = Q( sqrt( E_s / (2N_0) ) )\n\nFor example, if s1 and s2 have energy E_s, and orthogonal, then the difference in correlator outputs has variance 2N_0, mean difference sqrt(2E_s) if signals have matched filter output, but for orthogonal, the output for s1 when s1 sent is E_s, and for s2 is 0 + noise, so the two outputs are (E_s + n1, n2), and we choose the larger.\n\nP_e = P( n2 > E_s + n1 ) = P( n2 - n1 > E_s ) = Q( E_s / sqrt(2 * 2N_0) ) = Q( E_s / (2 sqrt(N_0)) ) since var(n2 - n1) = 2N_0.\n\nn1 and n2 are Gaussian with variance N_0, independent.\n\nSo n2 - n1 ~ N(0, 2N_0)\n\nP_e = P( n2 - n1 > E_s ) = Q( E_s / sqrt(2 * 2N_0) ) = Q( E_s / (2 sqrt(N_0)) ) = Q( sqrt(E_s^2 / (4 N_0)) ) = Q( (1/2) sqrt(E_s / N_0 * E_s / N_0) ) wait.\n\nQ( (E_s) / sqrt(4 N_0) ) = Q( (1/2) sqrt(E_s^2 / N_0) ) no.\n\n E_s / sqrt(4 N_0) = (1/2) sqrt(E_s^2 / N_0) not helpful.\n\nSo P_e = Q( E_s / sqrt(4 N_0) ) = Q( sqrt( E_s^2 / (4 N_0) ) ) = but it's Q( (E_s) / (2 sqrt(N_0)) )\n\nTo have this, we need E_s / (2 sqrt(N_0)) large.\n\nBut for M=2, we have two signals.\n\nFor M=256, it's the probability that one of the other 255 has larger output.\n\nFor signal i sent, output at i is E_s + n_i\n\nAt other j, n_j\n\nWe wrong if max_{j≠i} n_j > E_s + n_i\n\nP( max_{j≠i} n_j - n_i > E_s )\n\nThis is complicated.\n\nIt can be shown that P_e = \\int_{-\\infty}^{\\infty} [1 - \\Phi(x)]^{M-1} \\phi(x - \\sqrt{2E_s/N_0}) dx where \\Phi is cdf of standard Gaussian, \\phi pdf.\n\nFor large M, this is approximately 1 when E_s/N_0 is small, and small when E_s/N_0 is large.\n\nBut perhaps use the bound P_e ≤ (M-1) exp( - E_s / (4 N_0) ) / 2\n\nSet this equal to 10^{-5}:\n\n (255) / 2 * exp( - E_s / (4 N_0) ) < 10^{-5}\n\n so exp( - E_s / (4 N_0) ) < 2*10^{-5} / 255 = 7.84e-8\n\n so - E_s / (4 N_0) < ln(7.84e-8) = -16. 35\n\n so E_s / (4 N_0) > 16.35\n\n E_s / N_0 > 65.4\n\n then 10^6 * T > 65.4\n\n T > 65.4e-6 seconds = 65.4 µs\n\n not in options.\n\nThis is for the upper bound, so actual T can be less.\n\nFor example, if E_s / N_0 = 20, then exp(-20/4) = exp(-5) = 0.0067, times 127.5 = 0.85, which is not small.\n\nTo have the bound < 10^{-5}, need as above.\n\nBut the bound is loose.\n\nFor practical purposes, for M=256, to have P_e = 10^{-5}, we need quite high E_s/N_0.\n\nBut in option B, T = 3.125e-6, E_s/N_0 = 10^6 * 3.125e-6 = 3.125, which is very small.\n\nSo P_e is close to 1.\n\n not 10^{-5}.\n\nFor larger T, E_s/N_0 is larger.\n\nLook at option A: T = 5.125e-6, E_s/N_0 = 10^6 * 5.125e-6 = 5.125\n\nStill small.\n\nOption H: T = 4.5e-6, E_s/N_0 = 4.5\n\nSame.\n\nNone have T > 10 µs.\n\nMax T in options is 5.125 µs.\n\nSo E_s/N_0 = 5.125\n\nThen for M=256, P_e is very high.\n\nBut the option says P_e = 10^{-3} to 10^{-7}, so must be low.\n\nPerhaps for orthogonal signalling, with the given SNR, but maybe I have a mistake.\n\nAnother thought: \" if the signal is encoded into 1 to 256 orthogonal signals\" means that each quantized level ( there are 256 levels) is mapped to a different orthogonal signal.\n\n So the transmit energy is the same for each signal, and the symbol rate is the sample rate.\n\n so E_s = energy per symbol = s_i / symbol rate.\n\n symbol rate = f_s = 1/T\n\n so E_s = s_i * T\n\n N_0 = η\n\n E_s / N_0 = (s_i / η) * T = 10^6 * T\n\n as before.\n\nTo have P_e small, need large T.\n\nBut not in options.\n\nPerhaps the s_i / η = 10^6 is not for the symbol, but for the bit or something else.\n\nPerhaps s_i is the energy, not power.\n\nLet's check the notation: {(s_i) / (η)} = 10^6\n\n s_i might be energy, or power.\n\nUsually, in communication, s_i often denotes signal power, and η is noise power spectral density, so s_i / η has units of Hz, which is dimensionless in decibels.\n\nSo s_i is power.\n\nSo E_s = s_i * T_s\n\nYes.\n\nSo E_s / N_0 = (s_i * T_s) / N_0 = (s_i / N_0) * T_s\n\n and N_0 = η, so s_i / η = 10^6 (in Hz)\n\n So E_s / N_0 = 10^6 * T_s\n\n T_s = T (sample interval)\n\nSo as before.\n\nThen for binary PCM, the probability of error for each bit.\n\nFor part (b): \" the number of bits N and the probability of error if the quantized signal is encoded into a binary PCM waveform.\"\n\nFor binary PCM, each bit is transmitted, and with matched filter, P_e = Q( sqrt(2E_b/N_0) )\n\n E_b = energy per bit = s_i * T_b\n\n T_b = bit duration = T / N\n\n since N bits per sample, and sample interval T, so bit rate R_b = N / T\n\n so T_b = T / N\n\n so E_b = s_i * (T / N)\n\n N_0 = η\n\n so E_b / N_0 = (s_i / η) * (T / N) = 10^6 * (T / N)\n\n then P_e = Q( sqrt(2 * 10^6 * T / N) )\n\n for N=8, P_e = Q( sqrt(2e6 * T / 8) ) = Q( sqrt(250,000 * T) )\n\n T is in seconds.\n\n if T = 3.125e-6, then T / N = 3.125e-6 / 8 = 3.90625e-7\n\n then E_b / N_0 = 10^6 * 3.90625e-7 = 0.390625\n\n then 2E_b/N_0 = 0.78125\n\n P_e = Q( sqrt(0.78125) ) = Q(0.884) = 1 - 0.812 = 0.188 (approximately) — very high.\n\n not 10^{-5}.\n\n if we want P_e = 10^{-5}, then Q(z) = 10^{-5} implies z = sqrt(2E_b/N_0) = about 4.264, so 2E_b/N_0 ~ 18.19, so E_b/N_0 = 9.095\n\n so 10^6 * T / N = 9.095\n\n for N=8, T = 9.095 * 8 / 10^6 = 72.76 / 10^6 = 72.76 µs\n\n not in options.\n\n Similarly, for orthogonal signalling, to have P_e = 10^{-5} for M=256, need E_s/N_0 at least 10 or so, so T = 10 / 10^6 = 10 µs.\n\n not in options.\n\n So perhaps the s_i / η = 10^6 is not s_i power / η, but something else.\n\n Perhaps s_i is the energy per bit or per sample.\n\n But the notation {(s_i) / (η)} = 10^6, and typically s_i is power.\n\n Perhaps in some notations, s_i is signal strength.\n\n Another idea: \" the Nyquist rate\" for the signal from 0.4 to 4.4 kHz.\n\n If it's a bandpass signal, the minimum sampling rate is 2 * bandwidth = 8 kHz, as long as the band is below sampling rate and conditions.\n\n So Nyquist rate = 8 kHz.\n\n Sampled at four times that: 32 kHz.\n\n T = 1 / 32e3 = 31.25 µs.\n\n Closest option? Not in list. 31.25 not there.\n\n 3.125 is there — option B.\n\n 3.125 is 1/32e4 = 1/320,000.\n\n 32 kHz is 32,000 Hz.\n\n 3.125 µs is for 320,000 Hz.\n\n Perhaps it's 32 kHz, and they mean T = 31.25 µs, but option B has 3.125 µs, which is one tenth.\n\n Likely a typo, and it should be 31.25 µs, but it's written as 3.125.\n\n Or perhaps in the options, it's 31.25, but written as 3.125 by mistake.\n\n Because otherwise, no option has T around 30 µs.\n\n So perhaps option B is intended to be T = 31.25 µs, and it's typed as 3.125 by mistake.\n\n But in the option, it's written as 3.125 microseconds.\n\n Perhaps the frequency range implies something else.\n\n Another thought: \"frequency range 0.4 to 4.4 KHz\" — perhaps it's from 400 Hz to 4.4 kHz, so bandwidth 4.0 kHz, and the highest frequency is 4.4 kHz, so for baseband sampling, Nyquist rate is 8.8 kHz.\n\n But perhaps they consider the range start from 0, but it says 0.4 to 4.4.\n\n Perhaps in some contexts, \"frequency range 0.4 to 4.4\" means the bandwidth is 4.4 - 0.4 = 4.0 kHz, and they use 2* bandwidth as Nyquist r