      "generated_answer": "The channel matrix is given as:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}\n0.5 & 0.3 & 0.2 \\\\\n0.2 & 0.3 & 0.5 \\\\\n0.3 & 0.3 & 0.4 \\\\\n\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nEach row corresponds to the output probabilities given an input symbol. Since the inputs are equally probable (each with probability $ \\frac{1}{3} $) and we're using maximum-likelihood (ML) decoding, for each output, we decode to the input that maximizes the likelihood $ P(\\text{output} \\mid \\text{input}) $, i.e., the largest entry in each column.\n\nBut actually, ML decision rule selects the input that maximizes $ P(y|x) $ for received $ y $ — so we must consider, for each possible output, which input is most likely to have produced it (column-wise max).\n\nLet’s analyze the **columns** of the matrix (each column is the probability of outputs given an input):\n\n- Column 1 (input 1): [0.5, 0.2, 0.3] → max is 0.5 → correct decision\n- Column 2 (input 2): [0.3, 0.3, 0.3] → all equal, so any choice is as good; ML can pick any, but probability of error depends on correct choice — but since all are equal, we can assume correct decision is made (no basis to do otherwise)\n- Column 3 (input 3): [0.2, 0.5, 0.4] → max is 0.5 → correct decision\n\nWait — this is not the right interpretation.\n\nActually, the standard way: the matrix is $ P(Y|X) $, row is input, column is output? Or vice versa?\n\nLooking at the matrix as written:\n\nIt is written as three rows:\n\nRow 1: 0.5  0.3  0.2  \nRow 2: 0.2  0.3  0.5  \nRow 3: 0.3  0.3  0.4\n\nTypically, such matrices are arranged as $ P(\\text{output} | \\text{input}) $, so entry $ P(j|i) $ is the probability of output $ j $ given input $ i $. So the rows sum to 1:\n\nRow 1: 0.5+0.3+0.2 = 1.0  \nRow 2: 0.2+0.3+0.5 = 1.0  \nRow 3: 0.3+0.3+0.4 = 1.0 → yes.\n\nSo rows = input, columns = output.\n\nSo:\n- If input = 1, output probabilities: P(1|1)=0.5, P(2|1)=0.3, P(3|1)=0.2\n- If input = 2, P(1|2)=0.2, P(2|2)=0.3, P(3|2)=0.5\n- If input = 3, P(1|3)=0.3, P(2|3)=0.3, P(3|3)=0.4\n\nNow, maximum-likelihood decoding: for each *received output*, we choose the input $ i $ that maximizes $ P(y|i) $.\n\nSo we need to look at each **output** (column), and for each, find which input $ i $ gives the highest $ P(y|i) $, i.e., the maximum in that column.\n\n- Output 1 (first column): values are 0.5 (from input 1), 0.2 (input 2), 0.3 (input 3) → max is 0.5 → decide input 1\n- Output 2: 0.3, 0.3, 0.3 → all equal → can choose any. Assume we choose one without error, but since all equal, the best we can do is pick one — error probability is based on correctness.\n- Output 3: 0.2, 0.5, 0.4 → max is 0.5 → from input 2, so decide input 2.\n\nSo the ML decision rule:\n- If output = 1 → decide input 1\n- If output = 2 → all inputs equally likely to cause it → we can choose any, say input 1, 2, or 3. But we want to minimize error — best is to pick one arbitrarily. Because all probabilities are equal, the probability of correct decision given input $ i $ depends on what we do.\n\nBut since symmetric, suppose for output 2, we pick input 1 — then only input 1 benefits.\n\nBut better: to maximize probability of correct decision, for ties, we should pick uniformly or assume optimal tie-breaking. But since all are equal, no one is better.\n\nBut actually, for ML, it doesn't matter — we can assume that when there's a tie, we guess correctly with probability proportional to the input prior, but since all inputs are equally likely, and all P(output|input) equal, the posterior is flat.\n\nHowever, a common approach is to assume that in case of tie, the decoder chooses among the most likely inputs uniformly, but to compute the average error probability, we can assume that the decoder picks one input (say, the first among ties), but to minimize error, we can choose the input that maximizes the chance.\n\nBut since the prior is uniform, and likelihoods are equal, the correct decision probability when output=2 and multiple inputs are equally likely is just the probability that the transmitted input is one of those with max likelihood — but since all three are equal, all inputs give P(y=2|i) = 0.3, so all are maximum. So for output 2, any input could be chosen.\n\nBut if the decoder chooses, say, input 1 when output=2, then only if input was actually 1, decision is correct.\n\nSo probability of correct decision depends on tie-breaking.\n\nBut in standard problems, when all are equal in a column, and all inputs are symmetric, we can compute the probability that the actual input is a maximizer — but since all are maximizers, any decision will be correct with probability equal to the prior mass of the correct input among the tied ones — but since only one actual input, if decoder picks one arbitrarily, probability correct is $ \\frac{1}{k} $ where $ k $ is number of tied inputs.\n\nBut here, all three inputs have same likelihood for output=2: 0.3 → so all are tied.\n\nSo if the decoder, upon receiving output=2, selects one input (say, input 1), then the correct decision only if input was actually 1.\n\nSo probability of correct decision given output=2 is $ P(\\text{input}=1 | y=2) $, but under uniform prior, by Bayes, $ P(i|y) \\propto P(y|i) P(i) $, and since $ P(y|i) = 0.3 $ for all i, and $ P(i)=1/3 $, then $ P(i|y=2) = 1/3 $ for each.\n\nSo no matter which $ i $ the decoder picks, the probability that it is correct is $ 1/3 $.\n\nBut that's not efficient — but ML doesn't use prior, it just picks one of the maximizing inputs. But since all maximize $ P(y|i) $, any choice is valid ML.\n\nBut to compute error probability, we need to average over everything.\n\nSo better way: total error probability:\n\n$$\nP_E = 1 - P_C\n$$\n\n$$\nP_C = \\sum_{i=1}^3 P(i) P(\\text{correct decision} | i)\n= \\frac{1}{3} \\sum_{i=1}^3 P(\\text{decision} = i | i)\n$$\n\nWe must compute for each input $ i $, the probability that the ML decoder outputs $ i $ when $ i $ was sent.\n\nSo:\n\nCase 1: Input = 1 (i=1)\n\nThen output distribution: P(y=1|1)=0.5, y=2: 0.3, y=3:0.2\n\nNow, for each output, what does ML do?\n\n- y=1: as computed, column 1: [0.5,0.2,0.3] → max is 0.5 → input 1 — so decoder decides 1 → correct\n- y=2: column 2: [0.3,0.3,0.3] → all equal. Now, decoder will pick one. Suppose it picks one of the three. But to minimize error, we assume it picks correctly if possible, but since indistinguishable, it will pick input 1 with some probability.\n\nBut standard assumption: in case of tie, the decoder chooses one of the maximizing inputs arbitrarily — but since all three are maximizing, if it chooses uniformly at random among them, then P(choose 1 | y=2) = 1/3\n\nBut more commonly in such problems, it is assumed that the decoder can pick any maximizing input, and to compute the best possible performance, we assume that it can choose the correct one when there's a tie. But that's not realistic.\n\nActually, ML decoder doesn't know the correct input, so it cannot guarantee to pick the correct one in ties. So we have to assume a tie-breaking rule.\n\nBut in many textbook treatments, when computing error probability with ML and uniform inputs, they compute the probability of correct decision given input $ i $ as the probability that the output $ y $ is such that $ P(y|i) > P(y|j) $ for all $ j \\ne i $, plus in case of ties where $ i $ is among the maximum, and assuming that the decoder chooses correctly in ties.\n\nSome assume that in ties, the probability of correct decision is $ 1/k $ where $ k $ is the number of inputs tied for maximum.\n\nBut another approach: to **lower bound** error, or just compute based on strict maximum.\n\nBut looking at the symmetry of the problem, perhaps we can proceed.\n\nLet us assume that for output $ y=2 $, since all inputs are equally likely to cause it, the ML decoder can choose any input — but to compute the average error, we must fix a decision rule.\n\nLet us define the ML decision rule as: for each output, choose the input $ i $ that maximizes $ P(y|i) $. If multiple, pick the one with smallest index.\n\nSo:\n- y=1 → choose i=1 (since 0.5 is unique max)\n- y=2 → all tie → choose i=1 (smallest index)\n- y=3 → column: [0.2,0.5,0.4] → max is 0.5 → i=2 → choose i=2\n\nNow compute $ P_C = \\sum_i P(i) P(\\text{decide } i | i) $\n\nP(i)=1/3 for each.\n\nInput 1 sent:\n- P(decide 1 | 1) = P(y=1|1) + P(y=2|1) + P(y=3|1) but decide only if decision is 1\n- decision is 1 when y=1 or y=2 (from rule)\n- P(y=1|1)=0.5, P(y=2|1)=0.3 → total P(decide 1 | 1) = 0.5 + 0.3 = 0.8\n- But y=3: P=0.2, and decision is 2 → wrong\n\nSo P(correct | 1) = 0.8\n\nInput 2 sent:\n- P(decide 2 | 2) = P(decision is 2 | input=2)\n- From rule, decision is 2 only when y=3\n- P(y=3|2)=0.5 → so P(correct |2) = 0.5\n- y=1: P=0.2, decision is 1 → wrong\n- y=2: P=0.3, decision is 1 → wrong\n- y=3: P=0.5, decision is 2 → correct\n\nSo only 0.5\n\nInput 3 sent:\n- decision is 3 only if the decoder chooses 3 for some y\n- but according to our rule:\n  - y=1 → decide 1\n  - y=2 → decide 1\n  - y=3 → decide 2\n- so never decides 3!\n- so P(decide 3 | 3) = 0\n- so P(correct |3) = 0\n\nThat can't be right — error is very high.\n\nBut is that fair? Our tie-breaking is biased.\n\nFor output y=3: P(y=3|i): i=1:0.2, i=2:0.5, i=3:0.4 → so max is i=2, so must decide 2. Cannot decide 3.\n\nFor y=2: all equal, so can decide any. We decided 1, but could decide 3.\n\nTo be fair, we should assume that in case of tie, the decoder can choose any, but for input 3, it only gets a chance if when y=2, it decides 3.\n\nBut if we want to maximize the overall correct probability, we should in case of tie choose in a way that helps, but since inputs are symmetric and equally likely, perhaps we should choose uniformly in tie.\n\nLet us assume that when there is a tie, the decoder chooses uniformly at random among the inputs that achieve the maximum likelihood.\n\nFor output y=2: all three inputs have P(y|i)=0.3, so all are ML. So decoder chooses i=1,2, or 3 each with probability 1/3.\n\nFor other outputs, unique decision.\n\nSo decision rule:\n- If y=1 → decide i=1\n- If y=2 → decide i uniformly among 1,2,3\n- If y=3 → decide i=2\n\nNow, compute $ P_C $.\n\nP_C = (1/3) [ P(correct|i=1) + P(correct|i=2) + P(correct|i=3) ]\n\nInput 1 sent:\n- y=1 (P=0.5) → decide 1 → correct\n- y=2 (P=0.3) → decide 1 with prob 1/3 → correct with prob 1/3\n- y=3 (P=0.2) → decide 2 → incorrect\n- So P(correct|1) = (0.5)(1) + (0.3)(1/3) + (0.2)(0) = 0.5 + 0.1 + 0 = 0.6\n\nInput 2 sent:\n- y=1 (P=0.2) → decide 1 → incorrect\n- y=2 (P=0.3) → decide 2 with prob 1/3 → correct with prob 1/3\n- y=3 (P=0.5) → decide 2 → correct\n- So P(correct|2) = (0.2)(0) + (0.3)(1/3) + (0.5)(1) = 0 + 0.1 + 0.5 = 0.6\n\nInput 3 sent:\n- y=1 (P=0.3) → decide 1 → incorrect\n- y=2 (P=0.3) → decide 3 with prob 1/3 → correct with prob 1/3\n- y=3 (P=0.4) → decide 2 → incorrect\n- So P(correct|3) = (0.3)(0) + (0.3)(1/3) + (0.4)(0) = 0 + 0.1 + 0 = 0.1\n\nTherefore, total correct probability:\nP_C = (1/3)(0.6 + 0.6 + 0.1) = (1/3)(1.3) = 1.3 / 3 = 13/30\n\nThus, error probability:\nP_E = 1 - P_C = 1 - 13/30 = 17/30\n\nBut let's compute:\nP_C = 13/30 → P_E = 1 - 13/30 = 17/30\n\nBut is this correct? For input 3, when y=3, P(y=3|3)=0.4, but the ML decoder sees that P(y=3|2)=0.5 > 0.4, so it decides 2, not 3. So indeed, even though 0.4 is the second highest, it's not the maximum, so decoder does not consider it — only the input with highest P(y|i) for that y.\n\nSo for y=3, only input 2 has the highest at 0.5, so decoder must decide 2.\n\nSimilarly, for y=1, only input 1 has 0.5, others less.\n\nFor y=2, all equal, so uniform selection over all three.\n\nSo yes, the calculation seems correct.\n\nP_C = (1/3)(0.6 + 0.6 + 0.1) = 1.3 / 3 = 13/30\n\nThus P_E = 1 - 13/30 = 17/30\n\nSo answer is 17/30\n\nLooking at options, F is (17)/(30)\n\nBut let's double-check P(correct|3):\n\nP(correct|3) = P(y=1|3) * P(decide 3 | y=1) + P(y=2|3) * P(decide 3 | y=2) + P(y=3|3) * P(decide 3 | y=3)\n\nP(decide 3 | y=1) = 0 (decide 1)\nP(decide 3 | y=2) = 1/3\nP(decide 3 | y=3) = 0 (decide 2)\n\nSo: (0.3)(0) + (0.3)(1/3) + (0.4)(0) = 0 + 0.1 + 0 = 0.1 → correct.\n\nSimilarly for others.\n\nSum: 0.6 + 0.6 + 0.1 = 1.3, times 1/3 = 1.3/3 = 13/30 correct.\n\nError: 17/30.\n\nAlternatively, compute total error.\n\nBut seems consistent.\n\nSo the error probability is $ \\frac{17}{30} $.\n\n\\boxed{F}",
