    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "The voltage across a time-varying inductor is given by $ v(t) = L(t) \\frac{di}{dt} + i(t) \\frac{dL}{dt} $. Given $ L(t) = L_0 (t + \\tanh t) $ and $ i(t) = \\cos(\\omega t) $, we compute $ \\frac{di}{dt} = -\\omega \\sin(\\omega t) $ and $ \\frac{dL}{dt} = L_0 (1 + \\text{sech}^2 t) $. Substituting these yields $ v(t) = L_0 (t + \\tanh t)(-\\omega \\sin(\\omega t)) + \\cos(\\omega t) \\cdot L_0 (1 + \\text{sech}^2 t) $, which matches option C.\n\n\\boxed{C}",
