    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "The given differential equation is a linear nonhomogeneous second-order ODE with constant coefficients. The homogeneous part $ \\frac{d^2x}{dt^2} - 2\\frac{dx}{dt} - 3x = 0 $ has characteristic equation $ r^2 - 2r - 3 = 0 $, with roots $ r = -1, 3 $, so the homogeneous solution is $ x_h = c_1 e^{-t} + c_2 e^{3t} $, which matches options B, D, G. For the particular solution, the forcing terms are $ 10a \\sin t $ and $ b(2t + 1) $; using standard methods (undetermined coefficients), the correct particular solution form leads to terms involving $ -a(\\cos t - 2 \\sin t) $ and $ -b[(2/3)t + 1/9] $. Thus, Option B is correct.\n\n\\boxed{B}",
