    "prediction": "qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "The turns ratio is calculated as $ \\frac{13,200}{2,200} = 6.00 $. Using the open-circuit test data (2,200 V, 3.1 A, 1550 W) on the low-voltage side, the core loss resistance $ R $ and magnetizing admittance $ Y_{oc} $ can be found, while the short-circuit test gives $ r_1' + r_2 = \\frac{P_{sc}}{I_{sc}^2} = \\frac{2500}{(90.9)^2} \\approx 0.303\\ \\Omega $ and $ |Z_{sc}| = \\frac{210}{90.9} \\approx 2.31\\ \\Omega $, leading to $ |X_{sc}| \\approx \\sqrt{|Z_{sc}|^2 - R_{eq}^2} \\approx 2.28\\ \\Omega $. Option **G** matches these calculations closely with a correct turns ratio of 5.82 (likely a rounding discrepancy), but among all options, **A** has the exact turns ratio of 6.00 and consistent values.\n\nHowever, upon closer inspection, the turns ratio should be $ \\frac{13,200}{2,200} = 6.00 $, but the short-circuit current is 90.9 A on the high-voltage side (since $ I_{rated} = \\frac{200,000}{13,200} \\approx 15.15\\ A $), indicating confusion in test side assignment. Rechecking, if the short-circuit test is on the high-voltage side (210 V, 90.9 A), then 90.9 A is the rated current on the *low-voltage side* ($ \\frac{200,000}{2,200} \\approx 90.9\\ A $), so the test is on the low-voltage side. Thus, $ Z_{sc} $ is already referred to LV. Then $ R_{eq} = \\frac{2500}{(90.9)^2} \\approx 0.303\\ \\Omega $, $ |Z_{sc}| = \\frac{210}{90.9} \\approx 2.31\\ \\Omega $, so $ X_{eq} \\approx \\sqrt{2.31^2 - 0.303^2} \\approx 2.29 $. Option **G** provides $ r_1' + r_2 = 0.303\\ \\Omega $, $ |X_{sc}| = 2.28\\ \\Omega $, $ Y_{oc} = 1.41 \\times 10^{-3} $, $ b_{mag} = 1.374 \\times 10^{-3} $, $ X_{mag} = 727\\ \\Omega $, and turns ratio 5.82 – close to 6, possibly due to voltage ratio under load. Given consistency in calculations, **G** is the most accurate.\n\n\\boxed{G}",
