      "generated_answer": "The electrostatic energy of a point charge at the center of a conducting spherical shell is due to the interaction of the charge with the induced charges on the inner and outer surfaces of the shell. The correct expression accounts for the capacitance-like configuration between the point charge and the shell, leading to energy stored in the electric field between radii $ r_1 $ and $ r_2 $. The correct formula is $ \\frac{1}{8\\pi\\epsilon_0} q^2 \\left( \\frac{1}{r_1} - \\frac{1}{r_2} \\right) $, but among the options, the closest standard form is:\n\n$$\n\\boxed{D}\n$$\n\nOption D, $ \\frac{1}{4\\pi\\epsilon_0} \\cdot \\frac{1}{2} q^2 \\left( \\frac{1}{r_1} - \\frac{1}{r_2} \\right) $, matches the known result for the energy stored in this configuration.",
