    "global index": "MMLUPro_engineering_10194",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: A carrier with amplitude 4 volts and frequencyf_c= 2MHz is frequency modulated by a modulating signal m(t) = sin 1000\\pit. The amplitude of the modulating signal is 2 volts and the frequency deviation was found to be equal to 1 kHz. If the amplitude and frequency of the modulating signal are increased to 8 volts and 2 kHz respectively, write an expression for the new modulated signal.\n\nOptions: \nA. v(t) = 4cos(4\\pi × 10^6 t + 2 sin2\\pi × 2000t)\nB. v(t) = 8cos(4π × 10^6 t + 4 sin2π × 1000t)\nC. v(t) = 8cos(2π × 10^6 t + 4 sin2π × 2000t)\nD. v(t) = 8cos(4π × 10^6 t + 8 sin2π × 2000t)\nE. v(t) = 4cos(4π × 10^6 t + 16 sin2π × 2000t)\nF. v(t) = 4cos(4π × 10^6 t + 4 sin2π × 1000t)\nG. v(t) = 4cos(2\\pi × 10^6 t + 2 sin2\\pi × 2000t)\nH. v(t) = 4cos(4\\pi × 10^6 t + 4 sin2\\pi × 2000t)\nI. v(t) = 8cos(4\\pi × 10^6 t + 2 sin2\\pi × 2000t)\nJ. v(t) = 4cos(4π × 10^6 t + 8 sin2π × 2000t)\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "The original frequency deviation is 1 kHz with a 2V, 500 Hz modulating signal (since $1000\\pi t = 2\\pi \\cdot 500t$), so the frequency sensitivity $k_f = \\Delta f / A_m = 1000 / 2 = 500$ Hz/volt. When the amplitude increases to 8V and the frequency to 2 kHz (i.e., $m(t) = 8\\sin(2\\pi \\cdot 2000t)$), the new peak deviation is $k_f \\cdot 8 = 500 \\cdot 8 = 4000$ Hz, and the modulation index becomes $ \\beta = \\Delta f / f_m = 4000 / 2000 = 2 $. However, in FM, the modulated signal is $ v(t) = A_c \\cos\\left(2\\pi f_c t + k_f \\int m(t) dt\\right) $, and integrating $8 \\sin(2\\pi \\cdot 2000 t)$ gives a term proportional to $ -\\frac{8k_f}{2\\pi \\cdot 2000} \\cos(\\cdot) $, but in standard form, we express it as $ v(t) = 4 \\cos(4\\pi \\times 10^6 t + 4 \\sin(2\\pi \\times 2000 t)) $, because $ k_f = 500 $, so $ k_f \\cdot A_m / f_m = 500 \\cdot 8 / 2000 = 2 $, wait — actually, the integral of $ \\sin(2\\pi f_m t) $ produces a $ -\\cos $, but the standard FM expression uses sine in the phase: $ \\beta \\sin(2\\pi f_m t) $, with $ \\beta = \\Delta f / f_m = 4000 / 2000 = 2 $. But the phase term is $ \\beta \\sin(2\\pi f_m t) = 2 \\sin(2\\pi \\cdot 2000 t) $, and carrier amplitude remains 4V, frequency $ f_c = 2 $ MHz → $ 4\\pi \\times 10^6 t $. So the correct expression is $ v(t) = 4 \\cos(4\\pi \\times 10^6 t + 2 \\sin(2\\pi \\times 2000 t)) $.\n\n\\boxed{A}",
