    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "The signal $ m(t) = n(t) \\cos(\\omega_0 t + \\theta) $ represents a noise-amplitude-modulated carrier with a random phase $ \\theta $ uniformly distributed over $[- \\pi, \\pi]$. Since $ n(t) $ and $ \\theta $ are independent, the power in $ m(t) $ is given by $ E[m^2(t)] = E[n^2(t)] \\cdot E[\\cos^2(\\omega_0 t + \\theta)] $. We know $ E[n^2(t)] = \\sigma^2 $, and $ E[\\cos^2(\\omega_0 t + \\theta)] = \\frac{1}{2} $ due to the uniform distribution of $ \\theta $. Thus, the power is $ \\sigma^2 \\cdot \\frac{1}{2} = \\frac{1}{2} \\sigma^2 $.\n\n\\boxed{I}",
