    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: If the speed of an airplane is measured by means of a stagnation tube, what correction must be applied to the constant-density indication when the plane is cruising at 300 mph?\n\nOptions: \nA. - 10 mph\nB. 25 mph\nC. -30 mph\nD. -15 mph\nE. - 5 mph\nF. 10 mph\nG. 5 mph\nH. 0 mph\nI. 15 mph\nJ. -20 mph\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "The stagnation tube (or Pitot tube) measures the stagnation pressure and assumes constant density to calculate airspeed. At 300 mph, compressibility effects become significant, causing the indicated speed (based on incompressible flow assumptions) to be lower than the true airspeed. Therefore, a positive correction must be applied to the indicated speed to account for compressibility — meaning the actual speed is higher than what is indicated under constant-density assumptions.\n\nThus, the correct answer is a positive correction of about 15 mph.\n\n\\boxed{I}",
