      "generated_answer": "We are given the system function $ H(j\\omega) = \\frac{j2\\omega}{1 + 2j\\omega} $ and input $ V(t) = \\cos t $, whose Fourier transform is $ \\pi[\\delta(\\omega + 1) + \\delta(\\omega - 1)] $. The output in the frequency domain is $ Y(j\\omega) = H(j\\omega) \\cdot \\mathcal{F}\\{\\cos t\\} $, so we evaluate $ H(j\\omega) $ at $ \\omega = \\pm1 $.\n\nAt $ \\omega = 1 $: $ H(j1) = \\frac{j2(1)}{1 + 2j(1)} = \\frac{2j}{1 + 2j} \\cdot \\frac{1 - 2j}{1 - 2j} = \\frac{2j(1 - 2j)}{1 + 4} = \\frac{2j - 4j^2}{5} = \\frac{2j + 4}{5} = \\frac{4}{5} + j\\frac{2}{5} $.  \nThus, $ Y(j\\omega) $ has components at $ \\omega = \\pm1 $, and inverse transforming gives the output:  \n$ y(t) = \\text{Re}\\left[ \\left( \\frac{4}{5} + j\\frac{2}{5} \\right) e^{j t} \\right] = \\frac{4}{5}\\cos t - \\frac{2}{5}\\sin t $.\n\nThis matches option A.\n\n\\boxed{A}",
