ut among the options, H is the only one claiming full 50W delivery, which is correct for matched load.\n\nBut it says 4% reflected — inconsistency.\n\nPerhaps the \"4 percent\" is a mistake in the option.\n\nBut let's calculate for 75-ohm: Γ = (75-50)/(75+50) = 25/125 = 0.2, |Γ|² = 0.04 → 4% reflected.\n\nAh! So for the 75-ohm case, 4% is reflected.\n\nBut the question then says: \"Suppose a 50-ohm load is used instead\" — so now matched.\n\nSo for 50-ohm load, 0% reflected, 50W delivered.\n\nBut the options are phrased as statements about the 50-ohm case? Or the first?\n\nThe question is: \"Find the ratio... for a 75-ohm load\" — so first part.\n\nThen \"Suppose a 50-ohm load is used instead... what does it signify for an incident power of 50W?\"\n\nSo the \"what does it signify\" refers to the 50-ohm case.\n\nSo answer should be: 0% reflected, 50W delivered.\n\nBut no option says that.\n\nH says: \"4 percent of the power is reflected; source supplies all the 50W power to the load with 50 ohm\"\n\nThis is self-contradictory: you can't reflect 4% and supply all 50W.\n\nUnless \"source supplies\" means something else.\n\nBut standard: incident power is 50W, reflected is 2W (4%), delivered = 48W.\n\nSo if 4% reflected, delivered = 48W.\n\nH says 4% reflected but supplies all — impossible.\n\nOther options: G says 5% reflected means 47.5W delivered? But says 45W.\n\nList:\n\nA. 2% → 49W delivered (correct for 2% reflection: 1W reflected, 49W delivered)\n\nB. 11% → 44.5W? 11% of 50W = 5.5W reflected, delivered = 44.5W — matches\n\nC. 1% → 49.5W — matches\n\nD. 6% → 47W should be delivered, but says 44W — mismatch\n\nE. 7% → 3.5W reflected, 46.5W delivered — matches\n\nF. 8% → 4W reflected, 46W delivered — matches\n\nG. 5% → 2.5W reflected, 47.5W delivered, but says 45W — mismatch\n\nH. 4% → should be 48W delivered, but says supplies all 50W — mismatch\n\nI. 3% → 1.5W reflected, 48.5W delivered, says 47W — mismatch\n\nJ. 9% → 4.5W reflected, 45.5W delivered — matches\n\n