    "global index": "MedMCQA_1612",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: If the distribution of intraocular pressure (IOP) seen in 100 glaucoma patients has an average 30 mm with an SD of 1, what is the lower limit of the average that can be expected 95% of times -\n\nOptions: \nA. 28\nB. 26\nC. 32\nD. 25\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "To find the lower limit of the 95% confidence interval for the mean, we use the formula: mean ± (z × SEM), where SEM = SD/√n and z ≈ 1.96 for 95% confidence. Here, SEM = 1/√100 = 0.1, so the margin of error is 1.96 × 0.1 ≈ 0.196. The lower limit is 30 − 0.196 ≈ 29.8, which is closest to 30, but among the options, none are near 29.8 — suggesting a misunderstanding in the question.\n\nHowever, if the question is asking for the lower limit of the *data distribution* (not the mean), then 95% of values lie within mean ± 2SD: 30 ± 2 → 28 to 32. So the lower limit is 28.\n\n\\boxed{A}",
