 only pawns near d5 are the c-pawn (already moved), d4 was on d4, captured on c5.\n\nAfter move 11: d4c5 → d4xc5, so d4 pawn is gone.\n\nThe c4 pawn was on c4, moved to d5 on move 13: c4d5 → cxd5.\n\nSo that pawn is on d5, and Black's knight captured it: 14...Nxd5.\n\nSo Black's knight is now on d5.\n\nWhite must recapture.\n\nWhat can recapture? Only if a White piece can capture on d5.\n\nThe White queen can go to d5.\n\nThe knight on f3 can go to e5 or g4, not to d5.\n\nThe knight on c3 can go to d5 — yes! Nc3-d5.\n\nThat's a knight move.\n\nNc3 to d5 is a valid move.\n\nThe queen can also go Qd1 to d5.\n\nBut Nc3-d5 is better — developing and forking or pressuring.\n\nBut is there a pawn recapture? No, the e3 pawn can't capture — only if it were on e4.\n\nSo only pieces.\n\nNc3-d5 is possible.\n\nAlso Qd1-d5.\n\nBut Nxd5 is better — it recaptures and develops, potentially threatening the Black queen or other pieces.\n\nBut after Nc3-d5, Black can play ...Be6 or something.\n\nBut the simplest recapture is with the knight.\n\nIs there any other piece?\n\nBishop on f1 can't reach d5.\n\nSo options: Nc3-d5 or Qd1-d5.\n\nNc3-d5 is more harmonious — developing with tempo.\n\nBut let’s see the position.\n\nAfter 14...Nxd5, White plays Nc3xd5.\n\nThat’s the most natural move.\n\nQd1-d5 is also possible, but blocks the bishop and less effective.\n\nThus, the best move is Nxd5.\n\nThe move is c3d5.\n\nBecause knight on c3 takes knight on d5.\n\nSo c3d5.\n\nBut move is to d5, capturing the knight.\n\nIn coordinate notation: c3d5.\n\nYes.\n\nSo the correct move is c3d5.\n\n\\boxed{c3d5}",
