r third.\n\nBut here: G major to F major is a whole step down (major second down).\n\nAnd using alto clef instead of bass clef: bass clef has F on the fourth line, alto clef has C on the third line, so they are offset by three lines.\n\nThat’s a difference of a perfect 10th, so not compensating.\n\nWait—perhaps the only option that keeps the note positions unchanged is **A**: keep the clef the same, transpose to a minor second above.\n\nBut that changes pitch → positions must change.\n\nWait—no.\n\nAfter careful analysis: **there is a known trick in transposition: when you transpose a melody by a certain interval and change the clef so that the written pitch remains the same, the position stays**, but only if the clef shift is equivalent to the transposition.\n\nFor example, transposing a melody that is written in bass clef to a higher pitch using alto clef—then note positions may stay the same.\n\nBut in this case, the only option where the **position** might not change is if the transposition is by an interval that matches the clef shift.\n\nBut none match.\n\nAlternatively, consider: **transposing a melody from G major in bass clef to D major in treble clef**. G to D is a perfect fifth up. But in treble clef, D is on the second line, while in bass clef, G is on the second line from the top. So if you transpose up by a perfect fifth, D is on the second line in treble clef exactly where G was in bass clef — so the note position is the same.\n\nWait — that's **Option C**: transpose to D major, using the treble clef.\n\nOriginal: G in bass clef — second line from top.\n\nTransposed up a perfect fifth (G to D) — D in treble clef is also on the second line.\n\nSo the **written note D is on the second line in treble clef**, just as G was on the second line in bass clef.\n\nThus, the **position** on the staff remains unchanged — even though the key and clef changed, the note is written in the same place.\n\nFor example, if the original melody has a G on the second line in bass clef, when transposed to D major and written in treble clef, the corresponding melody note (now D) is also on the second line in treble clef.\n\nSo the staff position (line/space) is unchanged.\n\nThis is the only option where the transposition leads to the same staff position for the corresponding note.\n\nThe others require note movement.\n\nSo the answer is C.\n\nBut is it a whole step up? D major is two sharps, G major is one sharp — so G to D is a perfect fifth up, not a major second.\n\nOption C says: \"Transpose to D major, using the treble clef\" — it doesn't specify interval, but D major is a perfect fifth above G.\n\nAnd since the staff position (second line) is the same, that means the note positions are unchanged.\n\nTherefore, the answer is C.\n\nBut let's check option D: transpose down a major second using treble clef. G down a major second is F. In treble clef, F is on the fourth line, while in bass clef, G is on the second line — different positions.\n\nSo D changes position.\n\nA: transpose up a minor second → G to G# → G# is higher, so position changes.\n\nB: F major with alto clef — F is on the fourth line in alto clef, but G was on the second line in bass clef — different.\n\nThus, only C has the same positional alignment.\n\n\\boxed{C}",
