    "prediction": "qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "A diminished fourteenth spans 14 scale degrees and is enharmonically equivalent to a major seventh (since 14 - 12 = 2, but downward inversion logic applies). The inversion of a diminished fourteenth would be a compound interval that, when added to it, equals 15 (as intervals sum to 15 in compound inversions: e.g., 14 + 1 = 15), so the inversion should be a major second (or compound forms). An augmented ninth (D) is not the inversion of a diminished fourteenth because it is equivalent to an augmented second plus an octave, which does not properly invert the diminished fourteenth interval.\n\n\\boxed{D}",
