      "generated_answer": "The resolution from \"mi-la\" to \"re-la\" refers to the voice leading where the leading tone (mi) resolves to the tonic (do), and the supertonic (la in a minor context or scale degree 6) moves to the mediant or remains. In major keys, \"mi-la\" (E-A in C major) resolving to \"re-la\" (D-A) fits best in C major, where E (scale degree 3) moves to D (scale degree 2), and A (scale degree 6) stays as a common tone.\n\nThis voice-leading is characteristic of a I⁶₄–V–I cadence in C major, where the dominant (G) is approached with a 6/4 tonic.\n\n\\boxed{C}",
